<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siyaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 948–<a href="/years/972">972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Harsha (Paramara), Siyaka II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siyaka II, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, was a Paramara king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> who turned his family from Rashtrakuta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> into an independent power. In 972 he invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>, the Rashtrakuta capital, an event recorded by the poet Dhanapala that hastened the fall of the Rashtrakutas. He was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakpati-munja">Vakpati Munja</a> and Sindhuraja.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Siyaka was the son of Vairisimha II and is the earliest Paramara ruler known from his own inscriptions, which have been found in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. The Harsola copper plates, dated 31 January 949, show that he was already on the throne by then and suggest that he was a feudatory of the Rashtrakuta ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, although they also give him the title Maharajadhirajapati. The historian K. N. Seth believes his acceptance of Rashtrakuta lordship was nominal.</p><p>His own inscriptions call him Siyaka, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> prashasti and the Arthuna inscription name Munja's predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>. Modern historians call him Siyaka II to distinguish him from an earlier Siyaka I, whom some scholars consider fictional. Merutunga's Prabandha-Chintamani names him Simha-danta-bhata, and Georg Bühler suggested that his full name was Harsha-simha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Harsola plates record village grants to two Nagar Brahmins after a victorious campaign against one Yogaraja, perhaps a Chavda chief or the Chalukya chief Avantivarman Yogaraja II. The court poet Padmagupta, in the Nava-sahasanka-charita, credits him with defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Huna</a> princes, a victory the fragmentary Modi inscription also records. A war on the eastern frontier against the Chandela king Yashovarman appears to have ended in defeat for Siyaka.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a> in about 967, his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khottiga">Khottiga</a> fought Siyaka at Khalighatta on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada River</a>. Siyaka won, though he lost his Vagada feudatory Kanka in the battle, and he pursued the retreating army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>. At its height his kingdom stretched from Banswara in the north to the Narmada in the south, and from Khetaka-mandala in the west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> area in the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to Padmagupta, Siyaka was a Rajarshi, a king-sage, who retired as an ascetic and wore clothes made of grass. Dhanapala's Tilaka-Manjari suggests he was a devotee of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>. He and his queen Vadaja had two sons, Munja-raja and Sindhu-raja. Since Munja's earliest inscription is dated 974, Siyaka must have retired or died between 972 and 974. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate of 14 October 969 shows Garuda, the Paramara royal emblem, striking a snake.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siyaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death in 967 his brother Khottiga faced the Paramara ruler <b>Siyaka II</b> of Malwa, a former vassal, who invaded the Deccan and sacked Manyakheta in 972, an event recorded by the poet Dhanapala.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He took advantage of the sack of the Rashtrakuta capital Manyakheta by the Paramara <b>Siyaka II</b> in 972, overthrew the last Rashtrakuta king, Karka II, and then defeated the loyalist resistance led by the Western Ganga ruler Marasimha II, whose candidate Indra IV died in 982.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Siyaka</b>, also called Harsha, made the dynasty independent and sacked the Rashtrakuta capital Manyakheta in 972.</blockquote><a href="/state/paramara-dynasty">Paramara dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
